Track 1: Fort Polignac No-Man's Grid
- Story & Context: Liam O'Shea lost his mind and his passport when the caravan’s Bedford lorry broke its axle outside the decommissioned customs checkpoint at Fort Polignac, dumping crates of water-damaged Roland rack units into the baking red shale. While desert technician Zeinab soldered corroded patch bays using a butane torch and diesel exhaust, Liam blew raw, furious low-octave squalls on his baritone sax through a bullhorn to vent his cabin fever. The nomadic border guards didn't draw weapons; instead, they dragged out a generator, patched his brass horn into a salvaged 1993 Alesis reverb unit, and kickstarted a forty-eight-hour open-air rave under a sun that smelled of burnt insulation and wild thyme. Leaning over the razor wire with an acoustic guitar, Liam snarled the chorus that inaugurated the scene: "We drew our borders on the back of a cigarette packet, mate, and the wind took both."
